<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  SG Bus Timings for macOS: what the app is allowed to do, for release builds (the copy people
  install). DebugProfile.entitlements is the same for `flutter run` and debug builds, plus the one
  the Dart VM needs while debugging.

  The App Sandbox is OFF, on purpose, as it was for the Electron app. With it on, the app couldn't:
  - update itself: the updater unzips the new SG Bus Timings.app with ditto, and a small shell
    script swaps it in for the old one after the app quits (flutter-desktop-app-spec §9). A
    sandboxed app can't write to its own bundle or the folder it's in, and anything it starts
    (the script, ditto, xattr) is sandboxed too;
  - read what the Electron app kept: macOS would quietly move the app's files into
    ~/Library/Containers/org.uwuapps.sgbus/, so the settings file and stored data in
    ~/Library/Application Support/SG Bus Timings (spec §12) would be out of reach.
  The Mac App Store requires the sandbox, but this app is shipped from GitHub releases instead.

  The other entries only take effect if the sandbox (or the hardened runtime, for location) is ever
  turned on. They're listed so the app's needs are written down in one place:
  - network.client: bus timings, the map's tiles, updates;
  - network.server: the map's own small web server on 127.0.0.1, which serves the map's page and
    its tiles from the disk cache, so the map works offline;
  - personal-information.location: the location dot and the stops near you.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<false/>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.server</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.personal-information.location</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
